WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.365 --> 00:00:02.970
(mysterious music)

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.900
- [Randy] Our product testers come to work

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.980
just like anybody else.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.550
Their job here, though,
is problem solving.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.360
They're here to put their backs into it,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.760
little bit of elbow grease.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.640
The big difference is
that they are 600-pound

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.701
hungry grizzly bears.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:24.701
(mysterious music)

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.080
(energetic music)

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.840
- [Narrator] This is Randy.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.410
He works at the Grizzly
and Wolf Discovery Center

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.353
just outside the gates of
Yellowstone National Park.

00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:40.360
When bears become
habituated to human food,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.620
they often become dangerous
and are either killed

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.650
or put into captivity.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.520
So to keep bears and people safe,

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.090
Randy runs a program that tests coolers,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:55.490
trash cans and other food
storage containers on live bears.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.610
If the containers pass the test,

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.718
they're given bear-resistant
certification.

00:01:00.718 --> 00:01:02.610
(playful music)

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.060
- The bears here at the center

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.070
are all rescued bears from the wild.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.500
These are bears that
gained access to unnatural,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.010
unsecured foods and no
longer can live in the wild.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.630
We have seven grizzly bears total.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.660
They're super, super strong;
they're very, very smart,

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:18.900
but it's that sense of smell

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.580
that really gets them the food reward.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.480
Their job is to sniff out
weaknesses in products.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.393
They are the ultimate
testers here at the center.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.210
Once the product is
here, we bait the product

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.630
with the bear's favorite
food, whether it be fish,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.250
meat, peanut butter, honey.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.870
In a way, you could say
I'm the chef for the bears

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.130
since I'm preparing their favorite meals

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.700
to try to entice the bears
to get into those products.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.250
If it's a cooler, it requires padlocks.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:53.043
Put it out in the bear habitat,
and let the bears at it.

00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:03.200
(jazzy music)

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.940
Once the bears start the testing
process, the clock begins

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.840
and it's a one-hour timeframe
that the bears are given

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.689
in order to try to gain
access to that product.

00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:19.370
(jazzy music)

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.020
Some of the most common
techniques the bears use

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.570
when they're trying to get
in a product is the biting,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:28.500
the chewing, the clawing,
yeah, a little bit of smashing.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.360
But they also do what
we call the CPR method,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.350
so they're bouncing up and
down with their front paws,

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.833
anything to try to find that
weak point in that product.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.760
A lot of times they're gonna
get a little hole started

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:42.810
and then, literally from there,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.653
they're tearing the product apart.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.990
So it has to withstand that
one hour, and if it passes,

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:53.343
it then gets that certification
of being bear resistant.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.910
This cooler passed the test.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.980
Even though it got some
damage on the top lid,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.320
they were not able to get
through that outer layer.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.760
All the product testing is video taped,

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.120
so the manufacturers, they're
gonna review that footage.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.290
If a product fails, the
manufacturer's gonna have to

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.950
go back to the drawing
board and make some changes.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.023
That would be a fail.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.080
The product testing is open to the public,

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.420
and hopefully when they leave here

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.740
they have a better
understanding of how smart

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.793
and how strong bears are and
how food driven they are.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.320
There is a saying that a
fed bear is a dead bear,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.750
and it basically means that
when those bears get into

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:34.610
unnatural food sources,
they're gonna be hanging around

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.150
our houses, and they're more
prone to get into trouble.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:42.030
So working here at the Grizzly
Center for 20 plus years,

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.410
really I've learned to appreciate and love

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.610
the bears here and want to keep the bears

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.949
out in the wild where they belong.

00:03:49.949 --> 00:03:53.699
(gentle instrumental music)

